<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwango River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cuango River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kwango is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, about 1,100 kilometres long, that rises in the highlands of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and flows north, forming part of the border with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasai">Kasai</a>. Its valley was home to the kingdoms of Matamba, Kasanje and the Yaka and was a major route of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwango-river.jpg" alt="Kwango River" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brian Harrington Spier from Shanghai, China · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tazua.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
